<h1>app.Run() Middleware in ASP.NET Core</h1>

<p>
    <strong>app.Run()</strong> is used to create a terminal middleware in ASP.NET Core.
</p>

<p>
    Terminal middleware means the request pipeline stops at this middleware
    and no middleware written after it will execute.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Basic Syntax</h2>

<pre>
<code>
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Hello from app.Run()");
});
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How app.Run() Works</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Request enters the middleware pipeline.</li>
    <li>app.Run() handles the request.</li>
    <li>Response is returned to the client.</li>
    <li>The middleware pipeline stops execution.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Point</h2>

<p>
    <strong>app.Run()</strong> does not have a <strong>next</strong> parameter,
    therefore it cannot forward the request to the next middleware.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Example 1: Simple Response</h2>

<pre>
<code>
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Website is Running");
});
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Output</h3>

<pre>
<code>
Website is Running
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Example 2: Middleware After app.Run()</h2>

<pre>
<code>
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Pipeline Ended");
});

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("This will never execute");

    await next();
});
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Explanation</h3>

<p>
    The second middleware will never execute because
    <strong>app.Run()</strong> terminates the request pipeline.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Internal Request Flow</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Client Request
       ↓
Middleware
       ↓
app.Run()
       ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Difference Between app.Use() and app.Run()</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Feature</th>
        <th>app.Use()</th>
        <th>app.Run()</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Pipeline Continues</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>No</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Has next Parameter</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>No</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Middleware Type</td>
        <td>Normal Middleware</td>
        <td>Terminal Middleware</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Real Use Cases of app.Run()</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Maintenance Mode Page</li>
    <li>Final Response Middleware</li>
    <li>Minimal APIs</li>
    <li>Fallback Response Handling</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Point</h2>

<p>
    app.Run() is a terminal middleware in ASP.NET Core that handles
    the HTTP request and stops further middleware execution.
</p>

<hr class="mt-5 border-danger border-2" />

<h2 class="mt-5">Real Example: Maintenance Mode Page</h2>

<p>
    One of the most common real-world uses of <strong>app.Run()</strong>
    is to temporarily stop the application and show a maintenance message
    to all users.
</p>

<p>
    Since <strong>app.Run()</strong> is terminal middleware,
    the request pipeline stops immediately after this middleware executes.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Step 1: Open Program.cs File</h3>

<p>
    Open the <strong>Program.cs</strong> file of your ASP.NET Core MVC project.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Step 2: Add app.Run() Middleware</h3>

<p>
    Add the following code before:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.MapControllerRoute(
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Add this middleware:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Website is Under Maintenance");
});
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 3: Run the Application</h3>

<p>
    Run the application and open any URL.
</p>

<p>
    Every request will display:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Website is Under Maintenance
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Important Observation</h3>

<p>
    Controllers, views, and middleware written after
    <strong>app.Run()</strong> will not execute because
    app.Run() terminates the request pipeline.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Internal Request Flow</h3>

<pre>
<code>
Client Request
       ↓
app.Run()
       ↓
Maintenance Message Returned
       ↓
Pipeline Ends
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Program.cs Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();

app.UseRouting();

app.Run(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("Website is Under Maintenance");
});

app.MapControllerRoute(
    name: "default",
    pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");

app.Run();
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Important Interview Point</h3>

<p>
    app.Run() is commonly used to create maintenance pages,
    fallback handlers, and terminal middleware in ASP.NET Core.
</p>